\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Mapping The Warp of the Milky Way Galaxy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Patrick Kantorski
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
May 5, 2014
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Utilizing 3 hours of observation time, my lab parters and I collected data
 on the power spectrum of the High Velocity Clouds in the Milky Way galaxy
 using the radio telescope at the Leuschner Observatory.
 Our pointing ranged from 
\begin_inset Formula $l=75^{◦}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $b=+20^{◦}\rightarrow-20^{\text{◦}}$
\end_inset

 with each pointing collected with an integration time of about 4 minutes.
 After converting frequency information into velocities and distances, we
 convolved a Gaussian profile as the kernel to smooth the data and also
 removed any noise below temperatures of 0K.
 Using the Python module 'PyLab', we created an image plotting the distance
 from and height above the galactic center, mapping temperatures to color
 intensities.
 The result of the experiment shows an uneven distribution of H1 emissions
 above and below the galactic plane.
\end_layout

