\begin_layout Subsection
Mapping The Galactic Warp
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
External galaxies are often distorted by nearby galactic neighbors.
 To determine if the Milky Way galaxy is distorted or 'warped', my partners
 and I used the radio telescope at the Leuschner Observatory and profiled
 a section of the sky vertically slicing across the galactic plane.
 Hypothetically, a 'flat' galaxy would have emission peaks everywhere at
 galactic latitude 
\begin_inset Formula $b=0^{\circ}$
\end_inset

.
 To observe and display these distortions, we were only able to take one
 complete vertical profile of the Milky Way.
 If our galaxy does experience a warp, the result of this experiment should
 show an uneven distribution of H1-emissions along galactic latitudes above
 and below 
\begin_inset Formula $b=0^{\circ}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Background Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Hydrogen 21cm Line
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The intrinsic spin property of the electron in a Hydrogen causes the atom
 to emit a photon at precisely a distance known as the 21cm line or a frequency
 of 1420.4MHz.
 Additionally, the transition between the three-fold degenerate triplet
 states and the non-degenerate singlet state in Hydrogen atoms have an energy
 difference 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta E=h\nu\thickapprox5.874\times10^{-9}eV$
\end_inset

.
 This energy difference in transmitted by photons when the spin state of
 the electron flip from their alignment with the spin state of the proton
 to an anti-aligned position.
 Since the frequency of 1420.4MHz is in the radio wave band of the light
 spectrum, H1-emissions penetrate most of the dust along the line-of-sight
 and are not absorbed or scattered.
 Utilizing the 21cm line, radio astronomers can examine spectra line emissions
 and study the propagation of H1-emitted photons across the vacuum of space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Doppler Effect
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Doppler effect is the change in frequency of a wave or periodic event
 for an observer in motion relative to a source.
 An everyday example of the doppler effect can be examined when a vehicle
 emitting a siren or horn approaches, passes, and then recedes away from
 an observer.
 As the sound waves approach, the frequency of the sound increases, making
 the pitch of the sound also higher.
 As the vehicle recedes, the frequency and pitch diminishes.
 This is because each wave takes slightly less time to reach the observer
 than the previous.
 For light waves, the effect is the same.
 While photons travel through space, the color or frequency of the wave
 also stretches and contracts.
 making the waves "bunch together".
 On the contrary, if the wave source is moving away from the observer, the
 waves "spread out".
 This 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bunching
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

spreading
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of propagating light waves is known as red and blue shift.
\end_layout

