\begin_layout Standard
Additionally, the velocity of the observer and the source are determined
 by the medium through which the waves are transmitted.
 The total Doppler effect therefore results from the motion of the source,
 the observer, and the medium and each of these effects must be analyzed
 separately.
 To find the Doppler velocity of light waves, we can use Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:doppler"

\end_inset

 shown below.
 In this equation, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\nu=\nu_{obs}-\nu_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is the speed of light.
 However, for waves which do not encounter much of a medium like light from
 point sources, only the relative difference in velocities between the observer
 and the source have to be considered.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v_{doppler}=-\left(\frac{\Delta\nu}{\nu_{0}}\right)c\label{eq:doppler}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Galactic Coordinate Transformation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The galactic coordinate system is an astronomical coordinate system based
 on the position of the sun about the center of the Milky Way.
 Galactic longitude (
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

) measures the angular distance of an object eastward along the galactic
 equator from the galactic center.
 Galactic latitude (
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

) measures the angular distance of an object above the galactic equator
 with positive to the north and negative to the south.
 These coordinates are an analogous to terrestrial longitude and latitude
 and are usually measured in degrees.
\end_layout

