\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In the galactic coordinate system, because of the large distances, the Sun
 is placed at the origin.
 Galactic longitude (
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

) is measured from the direction from the Sun to the center of the galaxy
 on the galactic plane.
 Galactic latitude (
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

) measures the angle above the galactic plane.
 Additionally the region between the sun's orbit and the galactic center
 is known as the solar circle.
 (Image was taken from http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Galactic_coordinates.JPG
 on May 5, 2014.)
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig: Galactic coordinates"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In spherical coordinates, the position of stars are often expressed in right
 ascension and declination without use of a coordinate for distance.
 Because celestial objects are extremely far way, astronomers often have
 little to no information about their exact distances and therefore use
 only the object's direction.
 Additionally, the direction of fairly distant objects is the same for all
 observers and it is convenient to specify this direction with the same
 coordinates for every object.
 Using Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:spherical"

\end_inset

 shown below, we can convert from terrestrial latitude and longitude to
 spherical coordinates (
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha,\delta$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{x}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\cos(lat)\times\cos(long)\\
\cos(lat)\times\sin(long)\\
\sin(lat)
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:spherical}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The horizontal coordinate system is a celestial coordinate system that utilizes
 an observer's local horizon as it's fundamental plane.
 This coordinate system divides the sky into an upper hemisphere and a lower
 hemisphere.
 In the upper hemisphere, objects and stars are visible or within sight
 while in the lower hemisphere, the our view of the sky is blocked by the
 Earth.
 The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

great circle
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 separating hemispheres is called celestial horizon or rational horizon
 and the pole of the upper hemisphere is called the zenith.
 Using the spherical coordinates (
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha,\delta$
\end_inset

) and multiplying it by the rotational matrix shown in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:sphere convert"

\end_inset

, coordinates in terrestrial latitude and longitude can be converted into
 celestial coordinates (
\begin_inset Formula $az,alt$
\end_inset

).
 Additionally, this depends on the observer's terrestrial latitude 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{(\alpha,\delta)\rightarrow(az,alt)}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
-\cos(LST)\times\sin(\phi) & -\sin(LST) & \cos(LST)\times\cos(\phi)\\
\sin(LST)\times\sin(\phi) & -\cos(LST) & -\sin(LST)\times\cos(\phi)\\
\cos(\phi) & 0 & \sin(\phi)
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:sphere convert}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the Milky Way galaxy is extremely large and takes a long time to
 complete a full rotation, galactic coordinates do not change much on 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Earth-based
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 scales of time.
 To convert terrestrial coordinates to galactic, we can utilize the rotational
 matrix shown in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:HA, Dec to l, b"

\end_inset

.
 By multiplying spherical right ascension and declination coordinates by
 the rotational matrix shown below in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:HA, Dec to l, b"

\end_inset

, we can convert (
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha,\delta$
\end_inset

) coordinates to (
\begin_inset Formula $l,b)$
\end_inset

 coordinates.
 Since hour angle and declination are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Earth-based
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 coordinates, this conversion will only depend on the observer's terrestrial
 latitude.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R_{(\alpha,\delta)\rightarrow(l,b)}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
-0.054876 & -0.873437 & -0.483835\\
0.494109 & -0.444830 & 0.746982\\
-0.867666 & -0.198076 & 0.455984
\end{array}\right]\label{eq:HA, Dec to l, b}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Methods and Observations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Operating The Telescope
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To operate and control the 4.5m radio telescope at the Leuschner Observatory,
 my partners and I programmed and remotely ran a Python script on Leuschner's
 local computer via SSH.
 Since the H1-emission line is centered at about 1420.4MHz and is present
 within a 12MHz Nyquist zone, we chose to set our L.O.
 frequency to a range 12 Nyquist zones, or 144MHz, away.
 We also added an additional 6MHz, half the nyquist frequency, to center
 the H1-line.
 This made the center emission at a frequency of about 1270.4MHz.
 For our observations, we also programmed the telescope to take four separate
 measurements per pointing for the purpose of calibrating our data.
 The four measurements taken include a sample with the L.O.'s frequency set
 to 2MHz above the center frequency at 1272.4MHz, 
\begin_inset Formula $10dB_{m}$
\end_inset

, another with the frequency shifted 2MHz below at 1268.4MHz, 
\begin_inset Formula $10dB_{m}$
\end_inset

, and two measurements using a noise diode to add 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{noise}=100K$
\end_inset

 to the total system temperature 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{sys}$
\end_inset

 of each signal.
 When operating the telescope, we also had to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

home
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the telescope before each measurement to make sure that it was functioning
 properly.
 Homing the dish resets the positioning drives of the telescopes and returns
 the telescope to its default homing position (alt=90°, az=180°).
\end_layout

