\begin_layout Subsection
Signal Processing in Python
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Manipulating Collected Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observational data files collected from the radio telescope at The Leuschner
 Observatory are stored in a '.log' format.
 To open these files, I used the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ReadSpec
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 module provided on the UC Berkeley Undergraduate Radio Lab GitHub page
 and then performed a mean calculation on the data array.
 A mean was performed on the total dataset to reduce the size and preserve
 the signal information but in a more manageable format.
 To store data after calibration, I loaded the data into a '.npz' file with
 the information on the entry numbers, dates of observation, time stamps
 for each measurement, 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 coordinates, frequencies, and temperatures.
 After calculating velocities and distances, I created a separate program
 to create another '.npz' file to store additional information on the Julian
 dates, right ascension and declination coordinates, local standards of
 rest, velocities, and distances.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Median Boxcar Smoothing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another signal processing method that I used to clean and filter out bad
 data points from the raw data files is called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Median Boxcar Smoothing
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 This is done by running a median across data within a window of points
 before and after every data point.
 By taking the median value within a window across all of the data, a new
 array of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

smooth
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 data points positioned along the shape of the raw signal will be produced.
 Because the number of elements in our data is approximately 8192, I made
 the radius of my boxcar 11 points.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to remove bad data points, I first subtracted out the smoothed
 data from the raw data to normalize the signal about the frequency axis.
 Then, by placing a cutoff range along the power axis above and below four
 times the standard deviation of the total normalized signal, I was able
 to find the position of each invalid point that deviates significantly
 from the standard deviation value.
 Since both arrays were of the same dimension, the outlier positions should
 be the same for both datasets.
 Finally, I replaced the bad data in the raw data with data points taken
 from the smoothed data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Polynomial Curve Fitting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While calibrating the spectral lines, we also must remove the baseline from
 the final product in order to isolate the H1-emission signal.
 To do this, I used a 5th order polynomial curve to fit to the underlining
 baseline shape of the data.
 Additionally, this was done Python using the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

PolyFit
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 function in the Numpy module.
\end_layout

