\begin_layout Subsubsection
Calibration Process
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first step to calibrate for the spectral lines is to find the system
 temperature of each of the ONLINE and OFFLINE signals.
 As shown in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:System Temperature"

\end_inset

, by multiplying the noise temperature with the proportionality between
 the total sum of the CALON measurements and the sum of the difference between
 the CALON and CALOFF measurements, the system temperature of each measurement
 can be approximated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
T_{sys}=\left[\frac{\sum P_{j}^{CALON}}{\sum\left(P_{j}^{CALON}-P_{j}^{CALOFF}\right)}\right]\times T_{noise}\label{eq:System Temperature}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After, the next step in calibration is to isolate the shape of the ONLINE
 and OFFLINE, CALOFF signal.
 To do this, one of the measurements have to be scaled down to the same
 level as the other.
 This can be done by multiplying the OFFLINE dataset by the gain factor
 shown in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:Gain Conversion"

\end_inset

.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "fig:gain fix"

\end_inset

 shows the result of applying this equation to Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "fig:raw data"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
G=\frac{\sum P_{j}^{ONLINE,CALOFF}}{\sum P_{j}^{OFFLINE,CALOFF}}\label{eq:Gain Conversion}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

