\begin_layout Subsubsection
Error Propagation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When taking a long integration and combining spectra, assuming each spectra
 has statistically the same error 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta T_{sys}$
\end_inset

, then the error in the sum can be found as shown below in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:Error in Sum"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(error\, in\, sum)^{2}=\left[\sum_{0}^{N-1}(\Delta T_{sys,n})^{2}\right]=N(\Delta T_{sys})^{2}\label{eq:Error in Sum}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(error\, in\, average)^{2}=\frac{\left(\Delta T_{sys}\right)^{2}}{N}\label{eq:Error in Avg}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The average value of a dataset is equal to the sum divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
 This makes the error associated with averaging squared approximately equal
 to the error in the sum squared divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus the error in averaging is shown by Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:Error in Avg"

\end_inset

.
 After taking the square root of the equation, we can see how how the noise
 in the data measurements decreases with the square root of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, the number of times that a measurement is repeated.
 Applying this equation to the general form of Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:HI Temperature"

\end_inset

, we get the following expression.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\delta T_{sys}(\nu)^{2}=\left[\left(\frac{\delta P_{j}^{ONLINE,CALOFF}}{P_{j}^{ONLINE,CALOFF}}\right)^{2}+\left(\frac{\delta P_{j}^{OFFLINE,CALOFF}}{P_{j}^{OFFLINE,CALOFF}}\right)^{2}\right](T_{sys})^{2}\label{eq:Error of Seperate}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the statistical properties of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{j}^{ONLINE,CALOFF}$
\end_inset

and 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $P_{j}^{OFFLINE,CALOFF}$
\end_inset

are the same, we can simplify the expression and combine the contributions
 of the two spectra (see Equation 
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit

\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:Error Total"

\end_inset


\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
).
 Therefore, the noise in 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta T_{sys}(\nu)^{2}$
\end_inset

 increases by a multiplicative factor of 2 or the noise in 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{sys}(\nu)$
\end_inset

 by a factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Although the ONLINE measurements contain the desired signal information,
 it is false to assume that spending more time collecting ONLINE than the
 OFFLINE measurements will optimize the accuracy of the data calibration.
 The best results are obtained by spending an equal amount of time on each
 pointing.
 This is because equal amounts of integration time produces statistically
 equal amounts of signal noise per pointing.
 Furthermore, since there is no way obvious way around inducing some signal
 noise into our data calibration, it is impossible to generate a completely
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

noise-free
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 spectrum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\delta T_{sys}(\nu)^{2}=2\left(\frac{\delta P_{j}^{ONLINE,CALOFF}}{P_{j}^{ONLINE,CALOFF}}\right)^{2}(T_{sys})^{2}\label{eq:Error Total}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Cloud Velocities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the HI-line frequency is centered about 1420.4MHz, when the line appears
 at different frequencies, we can conclude that the signals must have been
 Doppler shifted.
 Using Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:doppler"

\end_inset

, the velocities corresponding to each frequency can be calculated.
 However, the return accurate Doppler velocity values, we much also correct
 our measurements to the Local Standard of Rest (LSR).
 The LSR is a velocity that accounts for the movement of our solar system
 about the galactic center.
 In our observation, our LSR correction ranged from -28.9693km/s to -35.7493km/s.
 Furthermore, to find the velocities corresponding to our line of sight,
 we must also divide our velocity by the cosine of the inclination angle
 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 (See Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:correct velocity"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v_{measured}=\left(v_{doppler}-LSR\right)\left(\frac{1}{\cos\left(b\right)}\right)\label{eq:correct velocity}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

