\begin_layout Subsubsection
Generating the Image
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the final calibrated data, we were able to construct an image.
 By averaging degenerate datasets, convolving them with a gaussian filter,
 and zeroing values with intensities below 0K, we were able to map intensity
 to the second plot shown on Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "fig:dist vs velocity & dist vs height"

\end_inset

.
 To do this, we first constructed a new image array with x values ranging
 from 0kpc to 25kpc and y values from -10kpc to 10kpc.
 Treating each square of information on this picture as a single pixel,
 the final picture came out to a resolution of roughly 0.1kpc/pix.
 After, data points were then divided by the resolution to fill every pixel
 on the array with information with intensities mapped to each pixel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
kernal=\exp-\left[\frac{x^{2}}{2\sigma_{x}^{2}}+\frac{y^{2}}{2\sigma_{y}^{2}}\right]\label{eq:kernal}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next step was to define weights for the image in order to keep track
 of the number of data points per pixel and create Gaussian kernel function
 to convolve or smooth the intensities (See Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:kernal"

\end_inset

).
 By convolving our image and then dividing it by the weights, we were able
 to essentially average our data points, bringing them to the corrected
 levels of intensities.
 To ensure that the image only convolves meaningful data points, if the
 pixel of information was not within a range of 3 pixels, the weights were
 set to 0K.
\end_layout

