\begin_layout Subsubsection
Basic Photometric Implications
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The optical depth of the H1-emissions along the galactic plane can be taken
 from the application of Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "eq:optical depth"

\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{actual}$
\end_inset

 is temperature intensity, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 is the optical thickness, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{area}$
\end_inset

 is the cross-sectional area of the H1 emissions, and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{emission}$
\end_inset

 is the column density or amount of particles per unit of volume.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(optical\, depth)=T_{actual}e^{-\tau},\quad\tau=\sigma_{area}N_{emission}\label{eq:optical depth}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Small values of optical thickness correspond to more information within
 the visible line of sight.
 In the case when the optical thickness 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\ll1$
\end_inset

, the optical depth reduces to the form 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{actual}\sigma_{area}N_{emission}$
\end_inset

.
 So, for optically thin regions, the optical depth is larger.
 When viewing Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "fig:final image"

\end_inset

, the regions through which H1 temperatures are plotted are optically thin
 while the lower temperature regions are optically thicker or experience
 more extinction as a result of dense H1 gas.
 The region around a distance of 10kpc looks like it may be extinction resulting
 from the spiral arms of our galaxy.
 Furthermore, because of the uneven distribution of H1 gas and uneven distributi
ons above and below the galactic plane, Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand vref
reference "fig:final image"

\end_inset

 supports the concept of galactic warping in the Milky Way.
 However, additional data collections may be required before a solid conclusion
 can be derived.
\end_layout

