\begin_layout Subsection
Troubleshooting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Several problems were encountered while trying to carry out this lab.
 The main issue was with data collection and resulted from an incorrect
 application of coordinate transformations.
 This was caught by me when I was calibrating data sets taken from May 1,
 2014 to May 3, 2014.
 If the telescope reaches the boundary of its pointing limits, the H1-line
 ends up skewed to positions where it should not be.
 After viewing this happening on a coordinate that was known to be within
 the pointing limits of the telescope, my partners and I immediately knew
 something was wrong.
 After fixing the rotational matrices in our observation code, my partners
 and I were limited to a 3 hour window on May 4, 2014 for data collection.
 Other groups performing experiments using the 4.5m radio telescope at the
 Leuschner Observatory were given more time for their observations based
 on necessity to complete their report.
 Since our experiment did not require as much data, the window of observation
 given to us was already small.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another issue we noticed was that some of our observations were returning
 empty values.
 To ensure this problem did not happen again, we took multiple vertical
 profiles for our last observation, reducing the resolution of our final
 image.
 Nevertheless, with 3 hours of observation time, we were still able to show
 evidence to support the galactic warp of the Milky Way galaxy.
\end_layout

